there's a new study from Yale University  the University of Florida and the  University of Maryland and what this  study says is that transitioning to a  single-payer health care system here in  the United States would save about four  hundred and fifty billion dollars per  year it would save the average family  $2,400 a year and it would prevent  68,000 unnecessary deaths representing  in total about a thirteen percent  savings in annual health care costs with  increased access now you all know that  I've talked a lot about Medicare for all  and sometimes my commentaries about  Medicare for all anger some people in  the audience and typically those  commentaries are ones where I make clear  that I support Medicare for all and I  believe it may well be the best system  but I believe that the practical  difficulties of making Medicare for all  a reality are often understated I'm a  realist  that's true and a lot of people hear my  realism and they call me a defeatist now  this is a realist show this is not a  show that gives you fantasies so we  discuss the difficulties of policy and  you might have noticed maybe you didn't  that congresswoman Alexandria Castillo  Cortez recently similarly said you know  even if Bernie's elected you don't just  wave a magic wand and get Medicare for  all she wasn't attacked for saying  basically the same thing as I did but  that's another story let's take some  time today to in a non hyper partisan  way think through this very good study  which shows that Medicare for all would  be cheaper and it would prevent a lot of  unnecessary deaths and let's apply it to  our thinking about health care all  together there's a great health care  scholar who you've maybe heard me  mention before he passed away not long  ago his name is who've a Reinhart okay  and in his writing ube would explain  that when you look at health care there  are essentially three factors to  consider cost access and quality okay if  you pick a certain health care cost and  imagine you say a hundred billion  dollars is our healthcare cost you can  then choose a balance between how much  access is there for a hundred billion  and how much quality is there for a  hundred billion so to make it visual  imagine you have a slider it said we  have a hundred billion dollars to spend  on healthcare and you have a slider and  you can either give that hundred billion  in health care spending to one person  right if you give the hundred billion to  one person you are very low access only  one person has health care but they're  gonna get everything they need for that  hundred billion dollars quality is going  to be absolutely huge or you go to the  other side and you say well let's give  five billion people coverage for that  hundred billion dollars in health care  spending and you can imagine how now  access has gone up to a hundred percent  but quality is going to go down you're  not if you split the hundred billion  among five billion people instead of  giving it all to one person the quality  of care is going to go down now that's  an extreme example to get us thinking in  this sort of three metric mode everybody  has health care so now imagine a  different slider we have a different  slider called 100% access okay in this  scenario everybody has health care but  now you've got a balanced cost and  quality right 100% access is fixed all  else being equal if you want quality to  be high with a hundred percent access  you're going to have higher cost if you  want cost to be low under 100% access  then you are going to have lower quality  right if five billion people get crappy  care you keep costs low to give five  billion people better care costs are  going to go up so I think you get the  idea these are the three metrics cost  access and quality and any healthcare  program this isn't even yet about  Medicare for all any system we have is  going to somehow balance cost access and  quality if you don't change the system  but you just give 