ow it works these are the three  factors and studies that say you can get  massive savings just from making some  small tweaks  you're often only talking about a couple  percentage points and case in point this  study has been analyzed by the Economic  Policy Institute and the Economic Policy  Institute said the cost savings that are  predicted are on the high side this is  not a surprise to us so again am i  against a system like Medicare for all  know I'm for it I'm for it I'm for it  I'm for Medicare for all I'm in favor of  Medicare for all what I'm telling you is  if you want to increase access to 100%  which I do if you want to keep quality  the same or increase it which I do it  most likely is going to cost much more  than what any candidate is currently  claiming and that's okay we can say as a  society it will cost more in total but  you'll save premiums and co-pays we'll  separate insurance from employers so  people don't have to tie their insurance  to a job will reduce the cost per person  rich people will have to pay quite a bit  more in order to subsidize the system in  total it will cost more I'm okay with  that  I'm okay because I think we need the  same or better quality than what we have  now and we need 100% access I'm ok  paying more if that's what it takes but  we need to understand the reality of the  plans and a lot of the plans are  assuming if we switch to Medicare for  All American health care costs per  capita will drop from what they are here  to what Australia or Canada pay that's  not going to happen by making the small  tweaks that a lot of people are talking  about it's gonna require much much more  yes we've got to eliminate waste but to  match the per capita costs of Australia  and Canada eliminating wet waste just  isn't enough and we've got to figure  that out and we need to have answers  because I want a system like this I hope  that my analysis is making sense I want  to hear from you remember I'm for  Medicare for all if you're